<header><p class="st-kicker">duchy · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Duchy of Legnica</h1><p class="st-meta">1248 CE – 1675 CE · capital Legnica</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The longest-lived Silesian Piast duchy, ruled from Legnica until 1675 and home to the first Protestant university project in Europe.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In the 1248 partition of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-silesia">Duchy of Silesia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boleslaw-ii-the-bald">Bolesław II the Bald</a>, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-ii-the-pious">Henry II the Pious</a> who died fighting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legnica">Legnica</a> in 1241, received the duchy around Legnica. His successors also gained <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brzeg">Brzeg</a> and at times <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wroclaw">Wrocław</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legnica">Legnica</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piast-dynasty">Piasts</a> accepted Bohemian overlordship in 1329 while keeping their ducal rule. The duchy prospered from agriculture, mining and cloth. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-ii-of-legnica">Frederick II of Legnica</a> introduced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Lutheran Reformation</a> in the 1520s and founded a university in 1526, the first Protestant one, though it soon closed. The dukes later adopted the Reformed faith and maintained a cultivated court at Legnica and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brzeg">Brzeg</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The last <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piast-dynasty">Piast</a> duke, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-william-duke-of-legnica">George William</a>, died in 1675, ending the dynasty that had ruled since the 10th century. The duchy passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> and in 1742 to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legnica">Legnica</a> has been part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> since 1945.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/duchy-of-legnica.jpg" alt="Duchy of Legnica" width="320" height="332"><figcaption>Image: Zuber · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Silesia_1290-1291.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1248 CE – 1675 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>duchy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Legnica</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Bolesław II the Bald, Frederick II of Legnica, George William of Legnica</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism, then Lutheranism and Reformed Protestantism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>German, Polish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Poland</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Duchy of Liegnitz</dd></dl></aside></div>
